I checked the actual source in [node_modules/@mantine/core/esm/components/Table/Table.mjs](node_modules/@mantine/core/esm/components/Table/Table.mjs:73-77) and can now answer this precisely rather than from memory.

There's no tree-shaking difference between the two styles for `Table`. Look at lines 12 and 73–81:

```js
import { TableCaption, TableTbody, TableTd, TableTfoot, TableTh, TableThead, TableTr } from "./Table.components.mjs";
...
Table.Td = TableTd;
Table.Th = TableTh;
Table.Tr = TableTr;
Table.Thead = TableThead;
Table.Tbody = TableTbody;
Table.Tfoot = TableTfoot;
Table.Caption = TableCaption;
```

`Table.mjs` unconditionally imports *every* subcomponent and attaches them as static properties the moment the module evaluates — it doesn't matter whether your code ever references `Table.Tbody`. As soon as you import `Table` at all (which you must, to render the `<Table>` wrapper), all of `TableTbody`, `TableThead`, `TableTr`, etc. are already pulled into the bundle. So:

- `Table.Tbody` — no extra cost, it's already bundled via `Table`.
- `TableTbody` (flat import) — same module, same code, just a separate named binding into the same already-bundled function.

The only scenario where tree-shaking would differ is if you used the flat subcomponents *without ever importing `Table` itself* — but nobody does that, since you need `<Table>` as the outer element.

What you might be remembering is a different, real effect: with a large barrel package like `@mantine/core`, tree-shaking matters a lot for *unrelated* top-level components — e.g. importing only `Table` keeps `Modal`, `Accordion`, etc. out of your bundle. That's a genuine and important effect, just not one that distinguishes `Table.Tbody` from `TableTbody` — those two are the exact same reference either way.

So the mixed usage in your codebase is a pure style inconsistency, not a bundle-size issue.
